            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Tutankhamun's Discovery</strong><br>\nThe discovery of Tutankhamun's tomb in 1922 by archaeologist Howard Carter brought the young Pharaoh into the spotlight. The tomb was filled with treasures, including the Pharaoh's famous gold mask, and revealed much about ancient Egyptian culture and burial practices. Unlike many royal tombs, it was found mostly intact, providing a wealth of artifacts for historians.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Early Life of Tutankhamun</strong><br>\nTutankhamun became a pharaoh at a very young age, around nine years old. His reign was during the 18th dynasty of Egypt's New Kingdom. As a boy king, he had advisors to help him rule, the most powerful being his vizier Ay, and the general Horemheb, who were likely the real powers behind the throne.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Tutankhamun's Reign</strong><br>\nDuring Tutankhamun's reign, he made decisions that restored the old Egyptian religion and its deities, which his father, Akhenaten, had replaced with the worship of a single god, Aten. This restoration was significant, as it returned Egyptian society to its traditional religious practices and stabilized the kingdom.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
